        public static List<TextEntry> Entries = new List<TextEntry>
        {
            new TextEntry{Title = "PREFACE",Content = "PREFACE \r\n\r\nThe translation of the Liturgy for the New Church\r\ninto any African language was first undertaken by the Rev. David William Mooki during the years 1923-26,and it was his intention to get it printed at his MissionPress at Krugersdorp. This, however, he could not do,owing to failing health , and when, on the 2nd April,1927, he was called on to Higher Service, it seemed all hopes of the African Liturgy were lost. But the Rev, Edward John Pulsford, who had already shown keen interest in this work, and who was very fond of the Sotho language, deyoted himself vigorously to the task of carrying on where Mooki had left, and with the Assistance of the Revs. Enos Pangwa and  Willie Lebona, and one or two laymen, he finally completed the translations by the end of 1928.\r\n\r\nThe work of printing the Sotho Service Book was achieved when, the Rey. P. H. Johnson B.A,, B.Sc. arrived in South Africa, although it contained onlythree services, in addition to the Morning Service. And with the coming of the Rev. Edwin Fieldhouse in 1936, the first New Church Hymnal was printed, and there was now a Service Book and a Hymn Book for use in the Mission. In this respect, the Rev, Ledima Seripe is to be especially mentioned, as he was of great assistance to Mr. Ficldhouse. He translated all the Sotho hymns from the English New Church Hymn Book.\r\n\r\nIn 1956 the Revs Brian Kingslake and Obed S. D. Mooki brought out 'Mo Roriseng - Jehova'  which contained hymns and liturgical matter in the FOUR main languages in use in the Mission. Needless to say, this was a very welcome publication, and it was used up to, and even after the Amalgamation in 1967 It is my very pleasant duty to present to the Amalga- mated Church in particular, and to the World in general, this Book of Worship, which has far more liturgical matter than any previous Service Book.and to which has been added a selection of Psalmsand 250 hymns.\r\n\r\nWe have not been unmindful of Buka ea Merapelo', which was printed and published by order of the Rt. Rev. Samuel Janmes Brander, Founder of the Ethiopian Catholic Church in Zion. That book, which was an adaptation of the Book of Common Prayer, was taken into consideration in the preparation of this Book of Worship, and the Secretary of the Committee responsible for its preparation, Rev. J. M. Mokhosi, served also on the cornmittee responsible for this Book of Worship. It will therefore be unnecessary for certain congregations to 1se one service book while others use another. Let there be only ONE Book of Worship, used by ALL Our ministers and congregations throughout the Church. May this Book of Worship be found useful, not only during Public Worship, but also in the homes of our members in The Committee appointed to carry through the task of cormpiling this Book of Worship are the following Ministers of the Church: JOHN OLDFIELD BOOTH,(Chairman) OBED SIMON DAVID MOOKI, (Secretary) JOSHUA WARWICK PHEELLO SHAI, JOALE MOSES MOKHOSI, GIDEON MEVANA BUTHELEZI and ALFRED ROBERT BRANDER. We wish to record a special word of appreciation for the work done by the Rev. 0. S. D. Mooki, who has in fact, done the actual work of translating, compiling and preparing the manuscript for the printer. May all who use this book look to the time when the Lord Jesus Christ shall indeed REIGN and 'All the world His Name adore'.\r\n         \r\nJohn Oldfield Booth,\r\n             \r\n General Superintendent.c" }

        };
    }
}
